A||||1||SLYGUY||Sudden stalling??!?!??!?!||||5||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||11-28-2001||03:42 AM||||This was a weird one...<P>On the way home from work a couple of days ago I exited the freeway and when I pulled up to the stoplight and pushed in the clutch pedal the car stumbled and then went out like a light. I restarted and it would idle between 400-600 rpm for a second or two then die!!<P>I was able to get it home, looked in the manuals I have. When that didn't help me a whole lot I disconnected the battery for a minute and then reconnected.<BR>Car ran lousy like I expected it to and still wouldn't idle till the computer took a hold of things. Then the car ran fine. Idle was a rock solid 800rpm. Idle would raise and then settle with the cycling of the A/C compressor also. <BR>Any ideas why it would do that and be fixed with a computer reset?<P>Sly<P>P.S. Within the last 2 months I have replaced:<BR>Spark plugs<BR>Distributor Cap/Rotor<BR>EGR<BR>O2<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||RossT||11-28-2001||04:03 AM||||My first thought is the IAC valve, but I would first clean out the throttle body, with<BR>TB spray cleaner. You should also pull the IAC and check to see if the tip is all gumed up.  Clean the tip, but don't spray the IAC main unit with cleaner.  Reinstall and run the car, try to get it above 35MPH or so.  When you reset the computer the car will use preset idle speeds until you drive over 35. If you still have a problem disconect the IAC wires to see if idle improves. <P>Also search the forum, there are lots of threads abouth this.  It could also be a bad TPS, vac. leak, bad injectors, bad ecm coolant sensor...  But search first and read the online service guide.  It tells you how to test many of the sensors.  Good luck!||||reg||1
